## <summary>qdistro admin broker SELinux types.</summary>
## <desc>
##   Dedicated SELinux types for the per-uid admin broker process
##   (qdistro-admin-broker.service).
##
##   Phase 1 (0.1.0) declared the types but left the broker exec
##   unlabelled — the broker still ran as `system_dbusd_t` via dbus
##   activation, and the dbus-chat interface forwarded to that type.
##
##   Phase 2 (0.2.0): labelled
##   /usr/libexec/qdistro/qdistro_admin_broker.py as
##   `qdistro_broker_exec_t`, added the init_daemon_domain transition
##   so systemd-started broker runs as `qdistro_broker_t`, switched
##   the qdistro_broker_dbus_chat() interface body to send_msg
##   against qdistro_broker_t. The broker was declared `permissive`
##   for the rollout pending audit2allow harvesting.
##
##   Phase 3 (0.3.0): dropped the permissive tag and added the allow
##   rules audit2allow harvested from a representative workload
##   (bats phase6.5 + phase6.6 + phase7 clipboard / handoff / qsu /
##   legacy clipboard probe paths) on val-260428-end. The broker
##   became properly confined.
##
##   0.3.1 (2026-04-29 late): added `dbus { acquire_svc }` against
##   `system_dbusd_t` because Tumbleweed's `dbus_system_bus_client`
##   interface — unlike upstream refpolicy — does NOT include
##   acquire_svc. Without it the broker died on startup with
##   `AccessDenied: Request to own name refused by policy` and no
##   AVCs. See gen_require + allow rule below.
##
##   0.3.2 (2026-04-29 evening): broker exec moved from
##   /usr/local/lib/qdistro/ to /usr/libexec/qdistro/ so the .fc rule
##   wins natively against Tumbleweed's `/usr/(.*/)?lib(/.*)?` →
##   `lib_t` glob.
##
##   0.4.0 (2026-04-30 — co-shipped with qdistro_pwd 0.2.0): the pwd
##   module's .fc labels the WHOLE /var/lib/qdistro/audit/ dir as
##   qdistro_pwd_audit_t (so the daemon's pwd_audit.sqlite picks up
##   the right type without a file_name_trans). The broker writes
##   audit.sqlite alongside in the same dir, so it now needs explicit
##   manage perms on qdistro_pwd_audit_t. Plus self:capability
##   sys_ptrace because the broker's caller-identity layering also
##   reads /proc/<other-uid>/exe for cross-uid clients.
##
##   0.4.1 (2026-05-01 — task(108)): grant
##   self:capability { dac_read_search dac_override } so the broker
##   (root in qdistro_broker_t) can traverse /var/lib/qdistro/audit/
##   (mode 0700, owned qdistro-pwd:qdistro-pwd by install-pwd-for-vm.sh)
##   and open audit.sqlite when install-pwd's `chown -R` reassigns its
##   ownership to qdistro-pwd. Without these capabilities the broker
##   dies on startup under enforcing with `sqlite3.OperationalError:
##   unable to open database file` — DAC blocks root before SELinux
##   even sees the file-class operation. The dir-search + file-rw
##   manage_*_perms from 0.4.0 are necessary but not sufficient: the
##   capability check fires *first*, and SELinux silences the file
##   access AVC because the open never happens.
##
##   Caller modules (tier1, future tier2/3 .te) don't change between
##   phases — only this module's body shifts.
##
##   0.5.0 (todo/fable-networking task 5, TCB no-network pilot): a
##   neverallow ratchet pinning OFF every network socket class for
##   qdistro_broker_t. The broker is AF_UNIX-only (system bus + hook
##   sockets) and never touches the network; the systemd unit now also
##   runs it under PrivateNetwork=yes + RestrictAddressFamilies. The
##   neverallow makes a future `allow` that re-grants a net socket fail
##   the POLICY BUILD (build ratchet), not silently widen the TCB —
##   copies the tier-2 module's narrowing pattern.
##
##   0.6.0 (todo/fable-networking task 5, VM-verified 2026-06-12):
##   CORRECT the 0.5.0 ratchet. VM assertion-checking (semodule with
##   semanage.conf expand-check=1) showed two defects: (1) the 0.5.0
##   comment claimed qdistro_broker_t "joins no network attribute" — it
##   was FALSE: the module called auth_use_pam(), which pulls the domain
##   into nsswitch_domain (via auth_use_nsswitch) and grants
##   self:{tcp,udp,netlink_route}_socket create. So the tcp/udp/
##   netlink_route neverallows were UNSATISFIABLE — the clean module
##   failed to load under expand-check=1. (2) With the distro default
##   expand-check=0, module neverallows are not checked at all, so the
##   0.5.0 assertions were cosmetic AND the loaded policy silently
##   granted the broker tcp/udp sockets via nsswitch_domain.
##   Root cause: auth_use_pam was the WRONG interface. The broker does no
##   PAM authentication — D-Bus peers are kernel-authenticated by
##   SO_PEERCRED; it only does NSS passwd/group *lookups*
##   (pwd.getpwnam/getpwuid, grp.getgrnam) for caller identity. Fix:
##   replace auth_use_pam(qdistro_broker_t) with auth_read_passwd() (read
##   of passwd_file_t — both /etc/passwd and /etc/group carry that type),
##   which does NOT join nsswitch_domain. That makes the FULL neverallow
##   over EVERY network socket class satisfiable again, so SELinux now
##   genuinely asserts the broker has no inet/raw/netlink-route sockets —
##   not just defence-in-depth on the exotic classes. The systemd layer
##   (PrivateNetwork=yes + RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_NETLINK ->
##   AF_INET socket() EAFNOSUPPORT, VM-verified) remains the primary
##   runtime guarantee. The build ratchet only has teeth under assertion
##   checking, so its test drives semodule with expand-check=1
##   (s56b-broker-no-network); VM-verified the clean module loads with 0
##   qdistro_broker_t neverallow violations and the broker still resolves
##   caller identity under enforcing with auth_read_passwd alone.
##
##   Spec refs: doc/selinux.md §"Broker module split-out";
##   doc/threat-model.md "TCB process discipline".
## </desc>
policy_module(qdistro_broker, 0.6.0)

########################################
#
# Declarations
#

# Broker process domain + the labelled exec. application_domain
# stays from Phase 2 (it pulls in the standard application allow-set
# the daemon needs to read /usr/lib/python*/site-packages, etc.);
# init_daemon_domain layers the systemd entry-point semantics on top.
type qdistro_broker_t;
type qdistro_broker_exec_t;
application_domain(qdistro_broker_t, qdistro_broker_exec_t)

# Per-uid runtime state under /run/qdistro/<uid>/* (audit DB,
# pending-request socket). Phase 2 declared the type but doesn't
# yet label /run/qdistro/ — a future bootstrap-side restorecon
# will pick this up once the .fc adds the runtime regex.
type qdistro_broker_runtime_t;
files_type(qdistro_broker_runtime_t)

# Phase 2: standard systemd-started long-running daemon. systemd
# spawns the unit with the labelled exec as the entry-point, the
# kernel applies init_daemon_domain's automatic type transition,
# and the broker process drops into qdistro_broker_t.
init_daemon_domain(qdistro_broker_t, qdistro_broker_exec_t)

########################################
#
# Phase 3 allow-set (audit2allow-derived from the val-260428-end
# representative workload).
#

# D-Bus client. The broker claims a well-known name on the SYSTEM
# bus (org.qdistro.AdminBroker1) and dispatches both inbound calls
# from qdshell / admin-app and outbound RulesReloaded / Decided /
# RequestPending broadcasts. The interface here covers the connectto
# + sock_file write + bus message forwarding leg.
dbus_system_bus_client(qdistro_broker_t)
# 0.3.1: Tumbleweed's selinux-policy ships a `dbus_system_bus_client`
# that does NOT include `acquire_svc` (unlike upstream refpolicy
# which lumps both into one interface). Without this explicit rule
# the broker can talk on the bus but cannot
# RequestName('org.qdistro.AdminBroker1') — dbus-broker rejects the
# request with `org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.AccessDenied: Request to
# own name refused by policy` and the broker dies on startup. The
# policy XML's `<allow own="...">` doesn't help: the SELinux dbus
# class check is enforced before the XML deny/allow decision.
#
# `gen_require` is needed because dbus_system_bus_client only pulls
# in the `send_msg` perm — `acquire_svc` is silently absent from the
# module's view of the dbus class on Tumbleweed.
gen_require(`
    class dbus acquire_svc;
')
allow qdistro_broker_t system_dbusd_t:dbus acquire_svc;

# /var/lib/qdistro/{audit,approvals,cache}/*.sqlite. The broker
# creates the dirs on first run (mode 0755, owner root) and writes
# .sqlite files with sqlite3's default "open + lock + write" pattern.
# Phase 3 stays on the generic var_lib_t label — a future Phase 4
# may declare qdistro_broker_var_lib_t and migrate. For now the
# inherited var_lib_t typing is fine because the directory tree
# under /var/lib/qdistro is broker-exclusive (only root + the broker
# domain ever touch it; no other refpolicy module manages it).
files_search_var_lib(qdistro_broker_t)
files_manage_var_lib_files(qdistro_broker_t)
files_manage_var_lib_dirs(qdistro_broker_t)

# Inotify on /etc/qdistro/rules.d/*.yaml. The dir itself is etc_t
# (the broker creates it via os.makedirs but the .fc doesn't
# label it — kept generic so admin can ship rules.d/ entries via
# normal config-management tooling without re-labelling). The watch
# permission is a single allow line; no upstream interface covers
# "watch a generic etc dir".
files_search_etc(qdistro_broker_t)
files_read_etc_files(qdistro_broker_t)
allow qdistro_broker_t etc_t:dir { watch read open getattr search list_dir_perms };
allow qdistro_broker_t etc_t:file watch;

# /proc/<pid>/{stat,exe,attr/current,cgroup} reads for caller
# identity layering (qdistro_admin_broker._caller_identity_layer).
domain_read_all_domains_state(qdistro_broker_t)
domain_getattr_all_domains(qdistro_broker_t)

# Audispd → broker AVC ingestion (spec/30 §audit). The broker
# follows /var/log/audit/audit.log via inotify when the audisp
# plugin is installed. logging_read_audit_log + the log dir search
# pattern.
logging_read_audit_log(qdistro_broker_t)
logging_search_logs(qdistro_broker_t)
# auditd-process /proc/<pid>/* reads (caller-identity layering walks
# every domain's process info). domain_read_all_domains_state above
# covers most of this; auditd_t needs its own gen_require because
# refpolicy treats it specially.
gen_require(`
    type auditd_t;
')
allow qdistro_broker_t auditd_t:dir search;
allow qdistro_broker_t auditd_t:file { getattr ioctl open read };
allow qdistro_broker_t auditd_t:lnk_file read;

# Python interpreter / dbus-send / similar shells the broker may
# fork+execve under nominal operation (e.g. an admin-app launcher
# spawn from the broker's RequestPending broadcast — actually those
# fork from qdshell, not the broker, but Python's import-time
# subprocess wrappers transitively need exec rights).
corecmd_exec_bin(qdistro_broker_t)

# tmpfs files (typically /run, /dev/shm). The broker creates and
# reads these via Python's tempfile module + multiprocessing.Queue
# style IPC (sqlite3 also memory-maps WAL files in tmpfs). The
# `execute` perm here covers Python's anonymous-mapping path for
# sqlite's PRAGMA mmap_size.
gen_require(`
    type tmpfs_t;
')
allow qdistro_broker_t tmpfs_t:file { execute read write };

# Self-signal + fifo for SIGHUP-driven reload (GLib.unix_signal_add).
# 0.4.0: sys_ptrace for cross-uid /proc/<other-uid>/exe readlink in
# the caller-identity layering (same kernel path as qdistro_pwd_t).
allow qdistro_broker_t self:process { signal sigkill sigchld getsched };
allow qdistro_broker_t self:fifo_file rw_fifo_file_perms;
allow qdistro_broker_t self:capability { sys_resource sys_ptrace dac_read_search dac_override };

# 0.4.0: shared audit dir. /var/lib/qdistro/audit is labelled
# qdistro_pwd_audit_t by the qdistro_pwd module's .fc; the broker's
# own audit.sqlite (NOT pwd_audit.sqlite — distinct filenames inside
# the same dir) inherits that label too. Allow manage on the dir +
# files so the broker can create + write its audit DB.
gen_require(`
    type qdistro_pwd_audit_t;
')
allow qdistro_broker_t qdistro_pwd_audit_t:dir  manage_dir_perms;
allow qdistro_broker_t qdistro_pwd_audit_t:file manage_file_perms;

# Caller-identity resolution. The broker maps uids<->names for its audit
# trail + rules engine via pwd.getpwnam/getpwuid + grp.getgrnam (NSS). On
# this image /etc/passwd and /etc/group are both passwd_file_t, so
# auth_read_passwd (read-only on passwd_file_t, plus init_dbus_chat for
# nss-systemd's varlink/D-Bus path) covers both.
#
# The broker does NOT do PAM authentication — D-Bus peers are kernel-
# authenticated via SO_PEERCRED, not pam_authenticate — so it deliberately
# does NOT call auth_use_pam(). auth_use_pam pulls the domain into
# nsswitch_domain (via auth_use_nsswitch) AND force-grants
# self:{tcp,udp,netlink_route}_socket create, which would defeat the
# no-network neverallow below (and made the 0.5.0 ratchet unsatisfiable).
auth_read_passwd(qdistro_broker_t)

# (0.6.0) The 0.5.0 module also carried optional systemd_dbus_chat_logind() and
# fprintd_dbus_chat() blocks "for the admin-approval auth path". The broker has
# no code path that chats logind or fprintd (no login1/fprintd references in
# broker/*.py) — that integration lives in the approval app, not the broker —
# so they are dropped for least privilege.

########################################
#
# TCB no-network ratchet (task 5, broker pilot)
#
# The broker talks ONLY over AF_UNIX (the system bus socket + its hook
# client/executor sockets, all filesystem paths) and reads /proc for
# caller identity. AF_VSOCK is not needed (unlike the print proxy). The
# ACTUAL no-network guarantee is the systemd unit: PrivateNetwork=yes +
# RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_NETLINK + IPAddressDeny=any, under
# which an AF_INET socket() fails EAFNOSUPPORT (VM-verified). SELinux is
# defence-in-depth on top of that.
#
# Scope of the neverallow: EVERY network socket class. This is satisfiable
# only because the domain joins NO network attribute — in particular it
# uses auth_read_passwd (file read), NOT auth_use_pam, so it is NOT a
# member of nsswitch_domain and does not inherit nsswitch_domain's
# self:{tcp,udp,netlink_route}_socket create grants (the defect that made
# the 0.5.0 ratchet both cosmetic and unsatisfiable — see the 0.6.0
# changelog). A future `allow qdistro_broker_t self:tcp_socket create`
# (here or in a child module) — or any interface that re-joins
# nsswitch_domain — then fails `semodule` expansion *when assertion
# checking is enabled* (semanage expand-check=1). The distro default is
# expand-check=0, under which module neverallows are NOT checked, so the
# build ratchet only has teeth under assertion checking; the no-network
# test (s56b-broker-no-network) drives expand-check=1 explicitly and
# proves the clean module is neverallow-satisfiable + an injected forbidden
# allow is rejected. Copies the tier-2 module's narrowing pattern.
#
# These socket classes are kernel base classes; name them in a require so
# the neverallow expression resolves regardless of what the joined
# baseline references.
gen_require(`
    class tcp_socket { create connect listen };
    class udp_socket create;
    class rawip_socket create;
    class sctp_socket create;
    class dccp_socket create;
    class icmp_socket create;
    class netlink_route_socket create;
    class netlink_tcpdiag_socket create;
    class packet_socket create;
')
neverallow qdistro_broker_t self:tcp_socket { create connect listen };
neverallow qdistro_broker_t self:udp_socket create;
neverallow qdistro_broker_t self:rawip_socket create;
neverallow qdistro_broker_t self:sctp_socket create;
neverallow qdistro_broker_t self:dccp_socket create;
neverallow qdistro_broker_t self:icmp_socket create;
neverallow qdistro_broker_t self:netlink_route_socket create;
neverallow qdistro_broker_t self:netlink_tcpdiag_socket create;
neverallow qdistro_broker_t self:packet_socket create;
